# Sorting

Imagine opening an online gradebook and choosing **“Sort by highest score.”** Instantly, the list rearranges so the top scores appear first, even though the same students are still in the class.

<img src="https://images.code.org/9d89fcfba48f603f4e8cee8b91e29cb4-image-1767804967209.png" alt="cartoon depiction of sorting a class by highest score" width="400px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 40px 40px;">

## Choose the Order

Sorting does not remove anything from the list. It changes what appears first.

**Before clicking each card, think about how the same data might feel different depending on the sort rule.**

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2, 1fr);gap:16px;margin:24px 0;">

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      🏆 Highest score first
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      This puts the strongest scores at the top.<br><br>
      <strong>What stands out?</strong><br>
      High performers are easiest to notice first.
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      📉 Lowest score first
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      This puts the lowest scores at the top.<br><br>
      <strong>What stands out?</strong><br>
      Students who may need support become easier to identify.
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      🔤 Name A to Z
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      This puts students in alphabetical order.<br><br>
      <strong>What stands out?</strong><br>
      The list feels neutral and easy to scan, but performance is not emphasized.
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;">
      🕒 Most recent submission first
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:12px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
      This puts the newest submissions at the top.<br><br>
      <strong>What stands out?</strong><br>
      Recent activity becomes easier to review first.
    </div>
  </details>

</div>

<div style="background-color:#B7E0E5;border-radius:30px;color:#000000;width:95%;padding:18px 24px;margin:24px 0;">

<p style="font-size:18px;line-height:1.5;margin:0;">
<strong>💡 Key Idea:</strong> Sorting changes the order, not the items. The sort rule shapes what users notice first.
</p>

</div>

---

## What is a sorting algorithm?

A **sorting algorithm** is a rule a program uses to put items in a particular order, like highest-to-lowest, A–Z, newest-to-oldest, or closest-to-farthest.

In JavaScript, sorting is often used with arrays, including arrays of objects, to organize data so it’s easier to read, compare, and make decisions.

<br>

<div style="text-align:center;width:80%;margin:0 auto;">
  <p style="
    font-size:18px;
    background-color:#F5F7F8;
    padding:12px 18px;
    border-left:5px solid #8148B2;
    border-right:5px solid #8148B2;
    display:inline-block;
    line-height:1.5;
  ">
   Every sort starts with an <b>order</b>.<br>
   Every order is a <b>choice</b> that changes what stands out first.
  </p>
</div>

---

## What is a compare rule?

When a computer sorts, it needs a way to decide which of two items should come first.

<div style="background-color:#B7E0E5;border-radius:10px;padding:15px;margin:20px auto;max-width:85%;box-sizing:border-box;word-wrap:break-word;overflow-wrap:break-word;">
<p style="font-size:14pt;margin-top:12px;line-height:120%;">
↕️ <strong>Compare rule</strong> is the logic a program uses to decide which of two items should come first when sorting a list.
</p>
</div>

A compare rule might be based on:

- Numbers, like highest to lowest  
- Text, like A to Z  
- Dates, like newest first  
- A specific property inside an object, like <code>score</code>, <code>lastName</code>, or <code>timestamp</code>  

---

## Quick Check

Think back to the sorting rules above.

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:12px 20px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
What changes when a list is sorted?
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:14px;background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:12px;padding:16px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
The order changes, but the items stay the same. Sorting rearranges the data instead of removing or adding items.
</div>
</details>

<br>

<details>
<summary style="cursor:pointer;display:inline-block;background:#8148B2;color:white;padding:12px 20px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;border-radius:999px;list-style:none;">
Why does the sort rule matter?
</summary>

<div style="margin-top:14px;background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:12px;padding:16px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.5;">
The sort rule changes what appears first, which can affect what users notice, compare, or act on.
</div>
</details>

---

